Common Amiga socket headers for the pre-1.0 IPv6 ABI correction
============================================================

Baseline: AmigaPorts/amiga-netinclude
Commit: c9fed00ba3186d9b02bc0de13eb0cf3c83f087e1
https://github.com/AmigaPorts/amiga-netinclude/pull/2

The baseline netinet/in.h, sys/socket.h, netdb.h and net/if.h retain the
upstream license notices and Latin-1 encoding; trailing spaces are removed.
New supplemental headers and Developer/support/in6_addr.c are MIT and
independently implemented. No generated library vectors change.

Put Developer/include before the compiler's NDK/netinclude directory. The
library, its applications and the Developer drawer use these same files; no
compiler cache is modified. This overlay can be replaced by an upstream pin.

All IPv6 applications built before 4.11 must be rebuilt. Established IPv4
layouts, vectors and options are unchanged.


AROS ALIGNMENT

Constants and shapes follow AROS commit 43e2e698c4075f8b9c19b05702ceca17b1ff5442
(workbench/network/common/include: netinet/in.h, sys/socket.h, netdb.h,
net/if.h). This is source-level alignment on the classic m68k ABI, not AROS
binary compatibility; no AROS code or LGPL header is copied.

  AF_INET6, PF_INET6       28 (23 remains AF_IPX); AF_MAX 29
  sockaddr_in6             28 bytes: len 0, family 1, port 2, flow 4,
                           addr 8, scope 24
  sockaddr_storage         128 bytes, len/family prefix, m68k-aligned
  IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS ..     4, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 (hops, multicast if/hops/
  IPV6_LEAVE_GROUP         loop, join, leave)
  IPV6_TCLASS              36
  IPV6_RECVPKTINFO         61
  IPV6_PKTINFO, HOPLIMIT,  46, 47, 37
  RECVHOPLIMIT
  if_nameindex             32-bit unsigned index and pointer


DELIBERATE EXCEPTIONS (need upstream agreement)

  IPV6_V6ONLY = 27   AROS assigns 26 to both V6ONLY and CHECKSUM. 26 stays
                     IPV6_CHECKSUM (declared, ENOPROTOOPT). Old Linux numeric
                     aliases are refused on every socket type.
  Resolver ABI       AI_NUMERICSERV stays 16 and EAI_* stay negative
                     (Roadshow/AmiTCP); 8 is already AI_EXT. AROS uses 8 and
                     positive values. Changing them would break IPv4 programs.


HEADERS AND SYMBOLS

Include <sys/socket.h>, <netinet/in.h>, <net/if.h> and <netdb.h>.
netinet6/in6.h and sys/cmsg.h are reached through the standard headers;
<aminetxduo/in6.h> remains a compatibility include.

AmiNetXDuo-specific opt-ins are separate from the common SDK:
  <aminetxduo/addrinfo.h>  AI_ADDRCONFIG = 0x0400 (AROS's value).
  <aminetxduo/cmsg.h>      IP_PKTINFO = 26, a 12-byte in_pktinfo payload
                          (ifindex, local address, destination), plus buffer
                          convenience macros. IP_RETOPTS stays 8.
  <aminetxduo/icmp6.h>     ICMPv6 filter API only. A complete standard
                          <netinet/icmp6.h> is outside this proposal.
Require AmiNetXDuo bsdsocket.library 4.11 or later before using these opt-ins,
or handle EAI_BADFLAGS / ENOPROTOOPT. Common netdb.h retains the stock flags
and AI_MASK: merely rebuilding an IPv4 application must not activate a flag
that Roadshow rejects. IPv4 users of pre-release IP_PKTINFO must rebuild.

in6addr_any and in6addr_loopback have external linkage. When the runtime does
not define them, compile Developer/support/in6_addr.c once into the program.
The shared library exports no data.


RUNTIME BEHAVIOUR

The explicit AI_ADDRCONFIG addendum filters DNS, numeric and null-node
results alike. Assigned non-loopback addresses count, IPv6 ULA and link-local
included (RFC 3493
6.1); tentative and unknown ones do not. Without the flag,
AF_UNSPEC hostname lookups query AAAA only when a 2000::/3 address exists;
AF_INET6 asks regardless. AI_V4MAPPED is undefined.
An AAAA and an A answer are ordered by RFC 6724 section 6 rules 1, 2, 3, 5,
6, 8 and 10, from the sources the stack would select. Rules 4 and 7 need
Mobile IPv6 and tunnel information the stack does not have; rule 9 compares
two destinations of one family, and a lookup returns one per family. An IPv6
destination with no usable source or route follows a usable IPv4 one; when
neither is usable, the order is kept.

To detect AmiNetXDuo, query SBTC_RELEASESTRPTR through SocketBaseTagList and
require success and an AmiNetXDuo release string, then check revision and
vector-table size with aminetxduo/capabilities.h. Other stacks and failed
queries disable private extensions. Developer/examples/IfNames.c shows the
sequence. Probe IPv6 itself with socket(AF_INET6, ...).


VERIFICATION

  python3 tests/toolchain/check_ipv6_sdk.py --toolchain /path/to/toolchain
      C89/C11/C++98/C++17, libnix/newlib/clib2, 68000/68020, both include
      orders, layouts, option collisions, in6addr_* linkage
  tests/ipv6/run-socket.sh -b BUILD -a ipv4|ula|linklocal|ipv6
      guest library calls, including AI_ADDRCONFIG before and after
      interface configuration
  addrinfo_dns_policy (host test)
      query counts and result order against controlled A/AAAA answers

Not tested: older GCC, VBCC and SAS/C executables. Open for upstream: the
V6ONLY allocation, and whether in6addr_* belong in the C runtime
or the SDK.
